        "Hebrew",
        """<strong>Sh’mu’el (<em>שְׁמוּאֵל</em>).</strong> "Heard of God" or "Name of God." Hannah will explain the name in 1:20 ("because I asked him of Yahweh"), playing on a different root (<em>sh’ul</em>, "asked") — a folk etymology rather than a strict philological one, characteristic of Hebrew naming. The book opens not with a king but with a barren woman in a distant hill-country village, embedding the start of the monarchy in domestic, marginal space — a literary move the book of Ruth has just modeled.""",
