    (
        5,
        3,
        "",
        "Dagon had fallen on his face to the ground before Yahweh’s ark",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Dagon prostrate.</strong> Dagon — the chief Philistine deity, originally a Canaanite-Amorite grain god (Hebrew <em>dagan</em>, "grain"; the popular fish-god etymology from <em>dag</em> is medieval folk derivation, not original) — is found face-down before the Ark in his own temple. The image is one of forced obeisance: an enemy deity adopting the posture of submission to Yahweh in the deity’s own sanctuary. The next morning the head and palms are severed, lying on the threshold (5:4), in deliberate echo of decapitated and dismembered enemy combatants in ANE warfare iconography (cf. 17:51, the decapitation of Goliath; Judg 1:6–7, Adoni-Bezek’s thumbs and toes). The Ark, which the Israelites had treated as a battlefield talisman in ch 4, now demonstrates that Yahweh’s power does not depend on Israelite custody — Yahweh fights from inside the enemy’s temple unaccompanied.""",
        "User original",
    ),
