    (
        11,
        6,
        "",
        "God’s Spirit came mightily on Saul",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The judge-Spirit.</strong> The phrase <em>tzalḥah ruaḥ Elohim</em> ("the Spirit of God rushed upon him") is the standard formula for the empowerment of judges — used of Othniel (Judg 3:10), Gideon (Judg 6:34), Jephthah (Judg 11:29), Samson (Judg 14:6, 19; 15:14). Saul is here cast as the last judge, and the Ammonite crisis as the last judge-deliverance, before the office formally transitions into kingship at 11:14–15. The same Spirit will later "depart" from Saul (16:14), with the result that an "evil spirit from Yahweh" troubles him — one of the OT’s most theologically uncomfortable passages, which the narrative places in deliberate parallel-and-inversion with this initial endowment. Saul’s tragedy is structurally framed by the Spirit’s coming and its withdrawal.""",
        "User original",
    ),
