    (
        11,
        7,
        "",
        "yoke of oxen and cut them in pieces",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The dismembered oxen.</strong> Saul’s gesture is a deliberate echo — and reversal — of Judg 19:29, where the Levite cut his concubine’s body into twelve pieces and sent the parts through Israel as a summons to inter-tribal action. The earlier mustering produced a civil war that nearly destroyed Benjamin; Saul, the Benjamite, repeats the gesture but with oxen rather than human remains, summoning Israel to defend a city (Jabesh-Gilead) that had ironically supplied the wives that saved Benjamin from extinction (Judg 21:8–14). The literary pattern is intricate: Saul’s first kingly act re-stages the trauma of Benjamin’s near-annihilation in a saving rather than fratricidal mode. The narrator is showing the reader, before any verdict on Saul’s reign is rendered, that he begins inside a long-running tribal memory.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        8,
        5,
        "",
        "a king to judge us like all the nations",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>"Like all the nations."</strong> The people’s demand contains its own theological problem — the request to be like the surrounding peoples is, in covenantal terms, a request to abandon distinctiveness. Deut 17:14–20 anticipates monarchy but with a sharply circumscribed king (no horses from Egypt, no many wives, must copy the Torah). The Deuteronomistic ambivalence is concentrated here: God grants the request despite warning against it. 1 Sam 8 stands as one of the canon’s most pointed political-theological texts.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        8,
        11,
        "",
        "the way of the king",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The royal critique.</strong> Samuel’s warning — sons conscripted, daughters drafted, fields taxed, harvests confiscated — describes Solomonic-era royal economy with disturbing accuracy. Many scholars read this passage as a retrospective critique composed under or after Solomon, retrojected onto Samuel. The same critique shapes prophetic literature against the monarchy (Hosea, Amos, Jeremiah). The verse is essential to any biblical political theology.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        10,
        1,
        "",
        "the vial of oil",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        """<strong>Mashaḥ (<em>מָשַׁח</em>) — "anoint."</strong> The verb that generates <em>mashiaḥ</em> ("anointed one"), Greek <em>christos</em>. Saul is the first <em>mashiaḥ</em> in the canonical narrative — a fact later messianic theology has had to reckon with. Anointing in the ancient Near East marked a person as ritually set apart for office (king, priest, prophet); the oil itself was understood to convey the office sacramentally. The trajectory from Saul’s anointing here to Christian "Christ" runs through David, the post-exilic high priest, the Qumran "Messiahs of Aaron and Israel," and ultimately Jesus.""",
        "User original",
    ),
