    (
        14,
        6,
        "",
        "no restraint on Yahweh to save by many or by few",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Jonathan’s theology of warfare.</strong> Jonathan articulates the foundational principle of Israelite holy-war theology: numerical odds are theologically irrelevant when Yahweh fights. The same principle drives Gideon’s reduction from 32,000 to 300 (Judg 7), David’s confrontation with Goliath (1 Sam 17:47), and the prayer at Mizpah (2 Chr 14:11). The contrast with his father is sharp: Saul, in 13:12, sacrificed prematurely from anxiety about troop numbers; Jonathan launches a two-man assault on a Philistine garrison from the same theology Saul could not hold. The chapter is partly a study in father-and-son contrast — Jonathan emerges as the Saul who might have been, and the narrative argument for his unfitness for the throne is undercut precisely by his fitness as a man of faith.""",
        "User original",
    ),
