    (
        14,
        24,
        "",
        "Cursed is the man who eats any food until it is evening",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Saul’s rash oath.</strong> Saul’s curse-by-fast is the OT’s third major rash-oath text, alongside Jephthah’s vow (Judg 11:30–40) and Israel’s sworn refusal to give wives to Benjamin (Judg 21:1–7). The pattern reveals Israel’s evolving juridical understanding of unbinding rash vows: Jephthah’s vow appears to be carried out (with the daughter unredeemed); the Benjamite oath is circumvented through the kidnapping at Shiloh; here, Saul’s oath would have killed Jonathan but the people redeem him by their counter-oath (14:45). Lev 5:4 will eventually codify the redemption of unwitting oaths through guilt-offering. The verse displays a king whose theological imagination runs to curses while his son’s runs to faith — and the army votes with its feet.""",
        "User original",
    ),
