    (
        14,
        45,
        "",
        "there shall not one hair of his head fall to the ground",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Popular nullification of the king’s oath.</strong> The people’s refusal to let Saul execute Jonathan is one of the OT’s rare instances of public override of royal command. The phrase "not one hair of his head" becomes proverbial (cf. 2 Sam 14:11, 1 Kgs 1:52, Acts 27:34) — a formal idiom of total preservation. The political stake is high: the army recognizes that Jonathan, not Saul, secured the day’s victory ("he has worked with God today"), and they treat this as morally outweighing the king’s sworn word. The episode foreshadows the larger pattern in which Saul’s personal commitments will be repeatedly overridden — by Samuel (15), by Yahweh (16), eventually by his own family’s loyalty to David (18–20). The throne is formally his and functionally compromised already.""",
        "User original",
    ),
