        "Easton.",
        "<strong>Dictionary (Easton's).</strong> <strong>EPHES-DAMMIM</strong> boundary of blood, a place in the tribe of Judah where the Philistines encamped when David fought with Goliath (1 Samuel 17:1). It was probably so called as having been the scene of frequent sanguinary conflicts between Israel and the Philistines. It is called Pas-dammim (1 Chronicles 11:13). It has been identified with the modern Beit Fased, i.e., “house of bleeding”, near Shochoh (q.v.). <em>[Reviewer: condense Easton's entry as needed.]</em>",
