    (
        21,
        6,
        "",
        "the show bread",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The bread of the Presence.</strong> The <em>leḥem ha-panim</em> (literally "bread of the face") was the twelve loaves placed weekly in the sanctuary (Lev 24:5–9), reserved for priestly consumption. Ahimelech’s decision to give it to David and his men crosses the cultic line for a humanitarian one — and Jesus cites this exact episode in the synoptic Gospels (Mark 2:25–28, Matt 12:3–4, Luke 6:3–4) as the textual ground for his disciples’ Sabbath grain-plucking. The Davidic precedent becomes Jesus’ paradigm for the principle that ritual law yields to human need ("the Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath"). The chapter that doomed the priests of Nob (ch 22) thus also became the OT episode most quoted in early Christian theology of law and mercy.""",
        "User original",
    ),
