    (
        21,
        13,
        "",
        "pretended to be insane",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The feigned madness at Gath.</strong> David, fled to the Philistine city of Gath itself (Goliath’s hometown!), is recognized and feigns insanity to escape — scribbling on the gate, drooling on his beard. The episode is unflattering by design: Israel’s anointed-future-king reduced to a public spectacle in enemy territory, surviving by performance. The superscription of Psalm 34 attaches to this episode ("a psalm of David, when he changed his behavior before Abimelech") — an attribution that gives the apparently humiliating scene a devotional afterlife. The narrator’s point is not the king’s heroism but his vulnerability: at this moment David is what Mephibosheth will later be (lame at the king’s table) — a man surviving on his protectors’ tolerance.""",
        "User original",
    ),
