    (
        23,
        6,
        "",
        "an ephod",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Abiathar and the ephod.</strong> The lone survivor of the Nob massacre flees to David with the priestly ephod — the divinatory instrument used for casting the Urim and Thummim (cf. Exod 28:30, Num 27:21). David thereby acquires institutional access to oracular consultation in a way Saul has lost (cf. 28:6, "Yahweh did not answer him by dreams or by Urim or by prophets"). The Saulide regime has lost legitimate cultic apparatus; the Davidic court-in-exile has it. Abiathar will serve David as priest until Solomon dismisses him in 1 Kgs 2:26–27 — explicitly described there as fulfillment of the curse on Eli’s house (1 Sam 2:31–35). The Nob slaughter, the ephod’s transfer, and Abiathar’s eventual dismissal form one continuous narrative thread spanning the books of Samuel through 1 Kings 2.""",
        "User original",
    ),
