    (
        23,
        18,
        "",
        "covenant before Yahweh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The third covenant — and the last meeting.</strong> David and Jonathan’s third recorded covenant (cf. 18:3, 20:16–17) and the final scene the two share. Jonathan crosses Saul’s sphere of authority to find David in hiding — a politically dangerous act, since failure of the search-party Saul has dispatched is then explained by Jonathan’s warning rather than incompetence. Their parting words assign the future plainly: "you will be king over Israel, and I will be next to you" (23:17). The pledge will not be kept, since Jonathan dies at Gilboa (31:2), but the underlying loyalty drives David’s long-term faithfulness to Mephibosheth (2 Sam 9). The scene is the closing frame of one of the OT’s most theologically rich friendships, registered with characteristic narrative restraint: the text gives no farewell speech, only the geographic separation that becomes permanent.""",
        "User original",
    ),
