    (
        24,
        6,
        "",
        "Yahweh’s anointed",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>"The Lord’s anointed."</strong> David refuses to kill Saul, even with Saul defenseless and even given the prophetic announcement of the kingdom-transfer. The principle — the anointed king’s person is sacred, even when the king is rejected — becomes foundational for later Davidic-messianic theology. The episode is set up as a moral test David passes; chapter 26 sets up an almost-identical scene to confirm the pattern. The principle is later weaponized politically (in 2 Sam 1, David executes the Amalekite who claims to have killed Saul) and theologically (the "anointed" / <em>mashiaḥ</em> trajectory toward NT christology).""",
        "User original",
    ),
