        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The law of equal shares.</strong> David’s ruling — that the two hundred who guarded the baggage at Besor receive an equal share of the recovered spoil with the four hundred who fought — becomes formally codified in 30:25: "he made it a statute and an ordinance for Israel, to this day." The principle reverses common ANE military practice (combatants outranking support troops) and grounds itself in Yahweh’s gift theology: the victory was not earned by the combatants alone but given by Yahweh to the whole company (30:23). The ruling has long been read as the OT’s closest analogue to wages-of-the-laborers economics, anticipating the principle of Matt 20:1–16 (the workers in the vineyard, all paid the same regardless of hours). The verse is a small but theologically potent text in early Israelite military and economic ethics.""",
