        "deep sleep from Yahweh had fallen on them",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The tardēmah from Yahweh.</strong> The Hebrew <em>tardēmah</em> ("deep sleep") is the same rare word used of the divinely-induced sleep that fell on Adam at the creation of Eve (Gen 2:21) and on Abraham at the cutting of the covenant (Gen 15:12). The vocabulary signals to the reader that David’s second sparing of Saul is providentially staged: Yahweh, not luck, makes the entire camp sleep through David and Abishai’s incursion. The narrative architecture is deliberate — chapters 24 and 26 are doublets, paired demonstrations that David refuses to harm Yahweh’s anointed even when offered the opportunity. The earlier scene relies on geographic accident (Saul enters the cave where David is hiding); this one relies on supernatural sleep. The doubling makes David’s restraint structural rather than incidental, foundational to the political-theological argument the books of Samuel are building about the legitimacy of his eventual kingship.""",
        "User original",
