        "I will now perish one day by the hand of Saul",
        "comm-modern-critical",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The crisis of Davidic faith.</strong> The verse is one of the rare windows the narrative gives into David’s interior life, and what it shows is theologically jarring: after the two miraculous sparings of chs 24 and 26, after Samuel’s anointing, after every prior demonstration of Yahweh’s preservation, David concludes that he will eventually be killed by Saul and decides on his own initiative to seek refuge among the Philistines. The decision contradicts everything the narrative has been arguing about David’s exceptional providential protection. Robert Alter and others read the verse as a deliberate portrait of faith’s failure under prolonged stress — the pious protagonist of chs 24 and 26 collapses into the political pragmatist who will spend sixteen months as a Philistine vassal (27:7). The narrator offers no judgment, but the structure of the book (David’s subsequent absence from the Gilboa battle by Philistine veto, ch 29) implicitly registers it: human despair occasions the providential rescue that David does not manage for himself.",
        "User paraphrase; summarises Alter",
