        "Saul has slain his thousands",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The song’s third appearance.</strong> The same verse the women sang in 18:7 — that triggered Saul’s descent into murderous resentment — now reappears in the mouths of Philistine commanders as the reason David cannot be trusted in battle alongside them (cf. its second appearance at 21:11, also in Philistine territory). The narrative pattern is theologically pointed: a song that began as celebration of Israelite victory has become so widely-known that even the enemy has internalized it as canonical fact. David’s reputation, established by women’s song outside political channels, has become the public truth Saul could not silence and the Philistines must now act on. The triple repetition of the same Hebrew couplet across three chapters with three different rhetorical functions (celebration, threat, military veto) is one of the OT’s clearest examples of a single textual fragment doing extended structural work in a narrative.""",
