        "Note",
        """<strong>The pivot at Ziklag.</strong> The verse is the moral hinge of David’s exile period and one of the OT’s great formulations of leadership under collapse. Returning to find Ziklag burned, families captured, and his own men talking of stoning him, David does not respond with the strategic pragmatism of ch 27 or the despair of 27:1 — he <em>strengthens himself in Yahweh his God</em>. The verb (<em>ḥazaq</em>, "to make oneself strong") is the same one Yahweh uses repeatedly with Joshua (Josh 1:6, 7, 9, "be strong and courageous"). David then immediately consults the ephod (30:7–8) — the legitimate oracular channel he has had access to since 23:6 but did not use during the Philistine sojourn. The verse marks David’s return to the providence-trusting mode that defined chs 23–26 and exited at 27:1. The pivot is what allows the entire reversal of fortune in 30:18–20 to be theologically intelligible: not luck, not strategy, but the king’s recovery of his proper posture toward Yahweh.""",
